  <h2>The Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Grounding — also called earthing — is the direct electrical contact between the human
    body and the surface of the earth. Grass, soil, sand, and natural bodies of water
    maintain a negative electrical charge. When your bare skin contacts these surfaces,
    free electrons transfer into the body, where they act as natural antioxidants — neutralizing
    positively-charged free radicals that drive inflammation and oxidative stress.
  </p>
  <p>
    For virtually all of human history, this contact was continuous. Modern life has
    eliminated it almost entirely: rubber and synthetic soles on every shoe, elevated
    structures, synthetic flooring, and a lifestyle pattern that rarely places bare feet
    on natural ground. Peer-reviewed research has documented measurable effects of grounding
    on inflammatory markers, cortisol rhythm, wound healing rate, and sleep quality.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is not a complicated intervention. The challenge is simply that modern life has
    engineered it out of existence. Restoring it requires intentionality.
  </p>

  <h2>The Insulting Behaviors</h2>
  <ul class="insult-list">
    <li>Wearing rubber-soled shoes exclusively — the most universal insulator between humans and earth</li>
    <li>Living and working on upper floors or synthetic flooring with no bare-ground contact</li>
    <li>Never spending time on grass, soil, sand, or natural water without footwear</li>
    <li>Urban-only lifestyle with no access to natural ground surfaces in daily routine</li>
    <li>Dismissing grounding as implausible without reviewing the indexed literature on inflammatory markers</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="section-eliminate">Eliminate the Insulting Behavior</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 1 — 20+ Minutes Barefoot on Natural Ground Daily</h3>
      <p>Grass, soil, sand, or concrete in direct contact with bare feet. Patio pavers do not conduct. This is the gold standard — free, zero side effects, requires only consistency.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 2 — Grounding Mat While Working</h3>
      <p>A conductive floor mat connected to the ground port of a standard outlet allows grounding while seated at a desk or workstation. No outdoor access required.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 3 — Grounding Sleep Mat or Sheet</h3>
      <p>A conductive sleep mat or fitted sheet provides 7–8 hours of passive grounding during sleep — the longest sustained period of electrical contact available.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 4 — Grounding Patches for Targeted Use</h3>
      <p>Adhesive conductive patches placed on the feet, wrists, or areas of chronic inflammation. Used during periods of extended indoor time or travel.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

